{% set local_problem %}
<p>A lot of Albertville homes have downspout problems that homeowners don't even realize. Downspouts that are too small for the roof area. Not enough downspouts for the gutter length. Extensions that have broken off or were never installed. Water dumping right against the foundation.</p>

<p>On Sand Mountain's clay soil, water doesn't soak in — it pools. When downspouts deposit water next to your house, that water sits there and eventually finds its way into your basement or crawlspace. Over years, this causes foundation settling, cracks, and expensive structural problems.</p>

<p>The fix is usually simple and affordable: properly sized downspouts, positioned right, with extensions that move water well away from your home. Basic stuff that pays for itself many times over.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three parts: enough capacity, good positioning, and proper extensions.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, big enough to handle your roof's water volume. North Alabama gets 55+ inches of rain per year — your downspouts need to handle heavy storms, not just light drizzle.</p>

<p>Positioning means putting downspouts where water naturally collects — at corners and low points in your gutter runs. Extensions (or underground drains for permanent solutions) then carry that water away from your foundation.</p>
{% endset %}

